{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Implemented the discriminator the previous interpretation named, and the draft-side run answers the question it was built for: the three rejections are not re-applied entries. The new operands report the node's applied_before and applying_before at the start of the apply batch plus reapplied_entry = (entry index <= applied_before). For seed 35: node 3 step 22 applied_before=3 applying_before=3 entry_index=4 reapplied_entry=false; node 1 step 23 identical; node 4 step 31 applied_before=0 applying_before=0 entry_index=4 reapplied_entry=false, i.e. a freshly rebuilt node consuming its log from the start. In every case the batch's entry index is applied_before+1 or the node's first entry, so the harness consumed committed entries in log order and did not re-present an already-applied entry - this refutes the double-application/off-by-one reading that the previous operands could not exclude, and leaves the target-side reading standing: a node consuming its own committed log in order is handed a joint-entering change at index 4, term 2 while its tracker is joint, with the Changer's only reaction being the contract's panic. Since these operands are draft-side only, the submission requests isolated execution of the harness with this instrumentation (applied_before, applying_before, reapplied_entry alongside the existing entry and node operands, with the counter and reasons still reported), states plainly what the discriminator can and cannot detect, and names the next missing operand: the type and term of the entries before the rejected index at the rejecting node, because that is what determines how the tracker became joint without a preceding leave and whether the proposal that put a second joint-entering change in the log was one the alreadyPending/alreadyJoint rewrite should have turned into a normal entry.\"}"}}
